The '''2013 Football League Cup final''' was a [[association football|football]] match between [[Bradford City A.F.C.|Bradford City]] and [[Swansea City A.F.C.|Swansea City]], which took place on 24 February 2013 at [[Wembley Stadium]] in London. It was the final match of the [[2012–13 Football League Cup]], the 53rd season of the [[Football League Cup]], a football competition for the 92 teams in the [[Premier League]] and the [[The Football League|Football League]].

Bradford City, of [[Football League Two|League Two]], were appearing in their first major cup final since they won the [[1911 FA Cup final]], and were the first fourth-tier side to reach the League Cup final since [[Rochdale A.F.C.|Rochdale]] in [[1962 Football League Cup final|1962]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/21022502 |title=Aston Villa 2–1 Bradford (3–4 agg) |work=BBC Sport|date=22 January 2013|access-date=23 January 2013}}</ref> Swansea City, of the [[Premier League]], were appearing in their first major English cup final in their 101-year history.<ref name="Report">{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/21489673|title=Bradford 0–5 Swansea|work=BBC Sport|date=24 February 2013|access-date=24 February 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Bantams battered by Swans |url=http://espnfc.com/uk/en/report/359954/report.html?soccernet=true&cc=5739 |work=ESPN |date=25 February 2013 |access-date=26 February 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130227021112/http://espnfc.com/uk/en/report/359954/report.html?soccernet=true&cc=5739 |archive-date=27 February 2013 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref>

Swansea won the match 5–0, and qualified for the [[2013–14 UEFA Europa League]], entering in the [[2013–14 UEFA Europa League#Third qualifying round|third qualifying round]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Nathan Dyer double helps Swansea ruin Bradford's Capital One Cup dream |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/feb/24/nathan-dyer-swansea-bradford-capital-one |work=The Guardian |publisher=Guardian News and Media |date=25 February 2013 |access-date=26 February 2013 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Who qualifies to play in Europe? |url=http://www.premierleague.com/en-gb/fans/faqs/who-qualifies-to-play-in-europe/ |work=premierleague.com |publisher=Premier League |access-date=24 January 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120907140644/http://www.premierleague.com/en-gb/fans/faqs/who-qualifies-to-play-in-europe/ |archive-date=7 September 2012 }}</ref> Although based in Wales, their participation in the [[English football league system]] means they took one of the English berths in the competition.<ref>{{cite news |title=Uefa give Swansea and Cardiff European assurance |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/17459332 |work=BBC Sport |date=21 March 2012 |access-date=24 January 2013 }}</ref> It was the first time the League Cup had been won by a non-English club and the first time a major English cup had been won by a non-English club since Swansea's [[South Wales derby|rivals]] [[Cardiff City F.C.|Cardiff City]] won the [[FA Cup]] in [[1927 FA Cup final|1927]].<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/league-cup/9891182/Bradford-City-0-Swansea-City-5-Capital-One-Cup-final-match-report.html|title= Bradford City 0 Swansea City 5|date=25 February 2013|work=Daily Telegraph|access-date=25 February 2013 }}</ref>

