Bradford City defeated [[Football League One|League One]] team [[Notts County F.C.|Notts County]] in the first round, winning in extra time through a [[James Hanson (footballer born 1987)|James Hanson]] goal.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/19123944|title=Notts County 0–1 Bradford|work=BBC Sport|date=11 August 2012|access-date=23 January 2013}}</ref>

Bradford's second round tie was against [[Football League Championship|Championship]] side [[Watford F.C.|Watford]], who went ahead after a 71st-minute goal from [[Ikechi Anya]]. Bradford scored, first through [[Kyel Reid]]'s 84th-minute equaliser and [[Garry Thompson (footballer born 1980)|Garry Thompson]] in injury-time.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/19340422|title=Watford 1–2 Bradford|work=BBC Sport|date=28 August 2012|access-date=23 January 2013}}</ref>

[[Burton Albion F.C.|Burton Albion]], also of League Two, played Bradford in the third round. Bradford were behind by two goals for the majority of the match, but [[Nahki Wells]] scored twice in the last 10 minutes, resulting in extra time. [[Stephen Darby]] scored the winning goal in the 115th minute.<ref>{{cite news |title=Bradford 3–2 Burton Albion |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/19632333 |work=BBC Sport |date=25 September 2012 |access-date=23 January 2013 }}</ref>

Bradford were drawn against [[Wigan Athletic F.C.|Wigan Athletic]] of the [[Premier League]] (who went on to win [[2013 FA Cup final|that year's FA Cup]]) in the fourth round. The match was goalless after 90 minutes and also after extra time, resulting in a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]]. Bradford won the shoot-out 4–2 with successful penalties from [[Nathan Doyle]], [[Gary Jones (footballer born 1977)|Gary Jones]], Stephen Darby and [[Alan Connell]]. It was the first time Bradford had knocked a Premiership club out of the competition since they defeated [[Nottingham Forest F.C.|Nottingham Forest]] in September 1995.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/20053638|title=Bradford 0–0 Wigan Athletic (2–4 pens)|work=BBC Sport|date=30 October 2012|access-date=23 January 2013}}</ref>

Bradford's quarter-final victory over [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] of the Premier League at [[Valley Parade]] was also decided on penalties, after the match finished 1–1 during regulation time. Nathan Doyle, Gary Jones and Alan Connell all successfully converted their penalties for Bradford, who won 3–2 on penalties.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/20590500|title=Bradford 1–1 Arsenal|work=BBC Sport|date=11 December 2012|access-date=23 January 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/20691424|title=Bradford v Arsenal in numbers|work=BBC Sport|date=12 December 2012|access-date=23 January 2013}}</ref>

In the semi-final first leg against Premier League [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]], Nahki Wells gave Bradford the lead after 20 minutes and [[Rory McArdle]] scored in the 77th minute. [[Andreas Weimann]] scored for Aston Villa in the 82nd minute, but [[Carl McHugh]] restored Bradford's two-goal lead in the 87th after heading home Gary Jones' corner.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/20880586|title=Bradford 3–1 Aston Villa|work=BBC Sport|date=8 January 2013|access-date=23 January 2013}}</ref> Aston Villa won the second leg 2–1, but Bradford won 4–3 on aggregate. [[Christian Benteke]] put Aston Villa ahead in the 24th minute, before Bradford's James Hanson levelled in the 55th minute. Andreas Weimann scored an 89th-minute goal for Aston Villa.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/21022502|title=Aston Villa 2–1 Bradford City|work=BBC Sport|date=22 January 2013|access-date=23 January 2013}}</ref>

===Swansea City===
