Swansea City received a bye into the second round as one of the thirteen [[Premier League]] clubs not involved in European competition. They were drawn against [[Football League Championship|Championship]] side [[Barnsley F.C.|Barnsley]] at the [[Liberty Stadium]]. Swansea won 3–1 after a 24th-minute opener from [[Danny Graham (footballer)|Danny Graham]] and two second-half goals from [[Luke Moore]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/19340378 |title=Swansea 3–1 Barnsley |work=BBC Sport|date=28 August 2012 |access-date=24 January 2013 }}</ref>

Swansea's third round opponents were [[Crawley Town F.C.|Crawley Town]]. Played at the [[Broadfield Stadium]], [[Michu]] put Swansea one goal ahead in the 27th minute. [[Josh Simpson (English footballer)|Josh Simpson]] and [[Hope Akpan]] put Crawley 2–1 ahead after 62 minutes. Graham levelled the tie in the 74th minute with header from a [[Dwight Tiendalli]] cross. [[Garry Monk]] scored an injury-time winner from a corner kick for Swansea. The match finished 3–2 and Swansea progressed to the fourth round.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/19632482 |title=Crawley 2–3 Swansea |work=BBC Sport|date=25 September 2012 |access-date=24 January 2013 }}</ref>

Swansea were paired with cup holders [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] at [[Anfield]] in the fourth round draw. [[Chico Flores]] put Swansea in the lead and [[Nathan Dyer]] scored a second goal for Swansea from a cross across the six-yard line from [[Pablo Hernández (footballer, born 1985)|Pablo Hernández]]. [[Luis Suárez (Uruguayan footballer)|Luis Suárez]] scored one goal for Liverpool, but a counterattack from Swansea led to a third goal for Swansea from [[Jonathan de Guzmán]] in the 90th minute.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/20053696|title=Liverpool 1–3 Swansea |work=BBC Sport|date=31 October 2012 |access-date=24 January 2013 }}</ref>

Swansea were drawn against Championship side [[Middlesbrough F.C.|Middlesbrough]] in the quarter-final. The only goal of the game came in the 82nd minute, after Middlesbrough defender [[Seb Hines]] headed the ball into his own net.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/20604705|title=Swansea 1–0 Middlesbrough |work=BBC Sport|date=12 December 2012 |access-date=24 January 2013 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/jan/23/swansea-chelsea-capital-one-cup|title=Swansea City have final say as Chelsea's Eden Hazard sees red|work=The Guardian |date=23 January 2013 |access-date=24 January 2013 }}</ref>

Swansea played [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] in the semi-final, with the first leg at [[Stamford Bridge (stadium)|Stamford Bridge]]. Michu scored in the 39th minute to give Swansea the lead, following a defensive error from [[Branislav Ivanović]]. A second mistake from Ivanović allowed Danny Graham to double Swansea's lead in the 90th minute; the match ended 2–0.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/20886408|title=Chelsea 0–2 Swansea |work=BBC Sport|date=9 January 2013 |access-date=24 January 2013 }}</ref>

The second leg was played at Swansea's [[Liberty Stadium]]. While Michu had the best opportunity to score in the 9th minute, the game finished 0–0 (2–0 on [[Playoff format#Total points series (aggregate)|aggregate]]) and Swansea advanced to the final. In the 80th minute, there was an incident between a [[ball boy]] and Chelsea player [[Eden Hazard]]. Replays showed that the ball boy was shielding the ball from Hazard in an attempt to waste time. Hazard then kicked the ball out from under the boy, prompting referee [[Chris Foy (referee)|Chris Foy]] to show him a red card for violent conduct.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/21038756|title=Swansea 0–0 Chelsea |work=BBC Sport|date=23 January 2013 |access-date=24 January 2013 }}</ref>

===Pre-match===
Before the match, it was announced that the English national anthem "[[God Save the King|God Save the Queen]]" would not be played before the final, as was usually traditional for the cup final. This was due to a Football League policy not to play the anthem when English and Welsh clubs met in a final.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.goal.com/en-gb/news/explained-why-when-god-save-the-queen-national-anthem-played-wembley-finals/bltb411c447b8775182|title=Explained: Why & when God Save the Queen national anthem is played before Wembley finals |date=24 May 2022 |accessdate=2025-09-12 |website=Goal.com}}</ref>

